3. WHAT NEEDS A PAID LICENCE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Organisational use requires a separate written commercial licence from the
rights holder. So does each of the following, whoever does it:

  (a) offering the Software or its functions to third parties as a service,
      hosted or otherwise;
  (b) selling, sublicensing, renting or distributing the Software for a fee;
  (c) embedding the Software or a derivative of it into a product or service
      supplied to others;
  (d) removing or obscuring the copyright and licence notices.

To ask for a commercial licence, contact the rights holder through
https://github.com/zarubinvibe

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. CONTRIBUTIONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Themiz is meant to be improved by the lawyers who use it. If you send a
contribution (a pull request, patch or any other material), you grant the
rights holder a perpetual, worldwide, non-exclusive, royalty-free, irrevocable
right to use, modify, publish and license that contribution as part of the
Software, including under the commercial licence in clause 3.

You keep authorship of your contribution. You confirm that you are entitled to
give these rights and that the contribution carries no third-party rights that
would prevent it.

Never include client data in a contribution: no case materials, no names, no
case-folder names, no registries. A case-folder name is a person's surname.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. NO WARRANTY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The Software is provided "as is". The rights holder gives no warranty of any
kind, express or implied, including fitness for a particular purpose.

Themiz does not practise law and does not give legal advice. It prepares
material for a lawyer. Every conclusion, every document and every decision on a
case remains the professional responsibility of the lawyer using it. Checking
what the Software produces before it goes to a court or a client is your duty,
not the machine's.

To the fullest extent permitted by law, the rights holder is not liable for any
loss arising from use of the Software, including a lost case, a missed
procedural deadline, an incorrect calculation, or disclosure of data through
